{"name":"Frosted Tulip","hex":"#f6d8d7","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"frosted","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Covered in frost; frosty.","Translucent, appearing to be covered in frost."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A kind of milkshake made with ice cream."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/frosted"},{"term":"tulip","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A type of flowering plant, genus Tulipa.","The flower of this plant."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tulip"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tulip","blocks":[{"text":"Tulips are spring-blooming perennial herbaceous bulbiferous geophytes in the Tulipa genus. Their flowers are usually large, showy, and brightly coloured, generally red, orange, pink, yellow, or white. They often have a different coloured blotch at the base of the tepals, internally. Because of a degree of variability within the populations and a long history of cultivation, classification has been complex and controversial. The tulip is a member of the lily family, Liliaceae, along with 14 other genera, where it is most closely related to Amana, Erythronium, and Gagea in the tribe Lilieae."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/%D7%A6%D7%91%D7%A2%D7%95%D7%A0%D7%99%D7%9D.JPG/330px-%D7%A6%D7%91%D7%A2%D7%95%D7%A0%D7%99%D7%9D.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tulip","wikidata":"Q93201"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Tulipa_alberti_Regel_-_PhytoKeys_%282024%29.jpg/500px-Tulipa_alberti_Regel_-_PhytoKeys_%282024%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Tulipa_alberti_Regel_-_PhytoKeys_%282024%29.jpg/1280px-Tulipa_alberti_Regel_-_PhytoKeys_%282024%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src"